<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Dukagjini</h1><p class="st-meta">1387 CE – 1444 CE (approx.) · capital Lezhë</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northern Albanian lordship of the Dukagjini family, whose name survives in the customary law code known as the Kanun of Lekë Dukagjini.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dukagjini were a noble family of the northern Albanian highlands who emerged as regional lords in the late 14th century. Around 1387 they held the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a> and its hinterland, between the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšić</a> lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> and the Kastrioti. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, they handed Lezhë to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1393.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Power rested on control of mountain clans between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> river and the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, with the family dividing lands among branches. The lords switched between Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> and Venetian protection, and were Catholic Christians linked to the bishoprics of the region. Highland society was governed by unwritten custom, later codified under the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leke-dukagjini">Lekë Dukagjini</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1444 Pal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leke-dukagjini">Lekë Dukagjini</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>, folding the lordship into the wider Albanian resistance. Lekë Dukagjini fought on after Skanderbeg's death until the Ottoman conquest of the late 1470s. The Dukagjini plateau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanun</a> still bear the family's name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-dukagjini.jpg" alt="Principality of Dukagjini" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dukagjini_(late_14th_century).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1387 CE – 1444 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lezhë</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pal Dukagjini, Lekë Dukagjini</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Albania, Kosovo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dukagjini lands</dd></dl></aside></div>
